function integrateScripts(scripts) {
  // Create a new array to store the generated script tags
  let scriptTags = [];

  // Loop through each script object in the given dataset
  scripts.forEach((script) => {
    // Check if the script has a type attribute, and default to "text/javascript" if not present
    const type = script.type || "text/javascript";

    // Create a new <script> tag with the appropriate attributes
    let scriptTag = `<script ${type}`;

    // Check if the script has an async attribute, and default to false if not present
    const isAsync = script.async ? ` async` : "";
    scriptTag += isAsync;

    // Check if the script has a defer attribute, and default to false if not present
    const isDefer = script.defer ? ` defer` : "";
    scriptTag += isDefer;

    // Check if the script has a nonce attribute, and default to an empty string if not present
    const nonce = script.nonce || "";
    scriptTag += ` nonce="${nonce}">`;

    // Add the script's innerHTML to the tag
    scriptTag += `${script.innerHTML}</script>`;

    // Push the generated tag to the array of script tags
    scriptTags.push(scriptTag);
  });

  // Create a new <footer> element and append all the script tags inside it
  let footer = `<footer>`;
  scriptTags.forEach((tag) => {
    footer += `${tag}`;
  });
  footer += `</footer>`;

  // Return the final HTML string representing the footer with all <script> tags included
  return footer;
}
```
This function uses the `forEach` method to loop through each script object in the given dataset, and creates a new `<script>` tag for each one. It checks for the presence of the `type`, `async`, `defer`, and `nonce` attributes and defaults to the appropriate values if not present. If any of these attributes are present, they are added to the generated tag using the appropriate syntax. Finally, it creates a new `<footer>` element and appends all the script tags inside it, returning the final HTML string representing the footer with all `<script>` tags included.

This function takes advantage of the `async` and `defer` attributes to load scripts asynchronously and optimize page load times. It also uses nonces to provide additional security against cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. However, it does not handle edge cases like invalid script objects or incomplete metadata, which could result in errors or unexpected behavior. Additionally, it may not be optimized for very large datasets due to its focus on initial load performance and minimizing memory usage.']

