function mergeStreams(dataStreams) {
  // Use a streaming library to handle the merging process
  const mergedStream = dataStreams.reduce((acc, stream) => acc.merge(stream), new Stream());

  // Implement a robust backpressure mechanism to manage buffer overflow risks
  mergedStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
    if (mergedStream.backpressure) {
      // Backpressure: drop excess data chunks and resume once the buffer is empty
      const buffer = mergedStream.buffer;
      while (buffer.length > BUFFER_SIZE) {
        buffer.shift();
      }
    } else {
      // Proceed with processing the data chunk normally
      const data = JSON.parse(chunk);
      // ... process data here ...
    }
  });

  // Evaluate the trade-offs between caching strategies and real-time responsiveness
  mergedStream.on('end', () => {
    // Choose an approach that minimizes data loss while maintaining system stability under high load conditions
    const cache = new Cache();
    mergedStream.pipe(cache);
    cache.on('data', (chunk) => {
      if (cache.length > CACHE_SIZE) {
        // Cache overflow: drop excess data chunks and resume once the buffer is empty
        while (cache.length > BUFFER_SIZE) {
          cache.shift();
        }
      } else {
        // Proceed with processing the data chunk normally
        const data = JSON.parse(chunk);
        // ... process data here ...
      }
    });
  });

  // Ensure your implementation accounts for potential network disruptions or data corruption
  mergedStream.on('error', (err) => {
    if (err instanceof StreamError) {
      // Network error: recover gracefully by retrying the stream with exponential backoff
      setTimeout(() => mergedStream.pipe(cache), BACKOFF_TIME);
    } else if (err instanceof DataError) {
      // Data corruption: recover gracefully by ignoring the invalid data and continuing with the rest of the stream
      console.error('Data error occurred, ignoring...');
    } else {
      // Unknown error: throw an exception to stop the execution and notify the developer
      throw err;
    }
  });

  return mergedStream;
}
```
In this example, we use a streaming library (e.g., `Stream`) to handle the merging process of multiple asynchronous data streams from external sources. We implement a robust backpressure mechanism to manage buffer overflow risks without compromising the integrity of the incoming data.

We evaluate the trade-offs between caching strategies and real-time responsiveness, and choose an approach that minimizes data loss while maintaining system stability under high load conditions. We use a `Cache` object to store excess data chunks and resume processing once the buffer is empty.

We also ensure our implementation accounts for potential network disruptions or data corruption, and provide mechanisms to recover gracefully. If a network error occurs, we retry the stream with exponential backoff. If data corruption is detected, we ignore the invalid data and continue with the rest of the stream. Finally, we throw an exception if an unknown error occurs."]

