The function `init_frames()` initializes and manages the dynamic array of frames. It sets all frames as invalid by default and resets the average signal strength to zero.

The function `add_frame()` adds a new frame to the dynamic array. It updates the number of valid frames and stores the new frame header, signal strength, and timestamp in the next available slot in the array. The function also handles potential out-of-memory conditions by checking the return value of `realloc()`.

The function `update_signal_strength()` updates the signal strength indicator based on recent symbol data. It calculates the average signal strength over all valid frames and resets the timestamp of the last update to the current time.

The function `get_frames()` retrieves frames based on certain criteria such as signal strength or time of reception. It locks the access to the array, iterates over all frames, checks if the frame is valid, and increments the number of valid frames if it meets the specified criteria. The function also handles potential errors and exceptions by calling `error_handler()`.

The main function continuously receives new frames and adds them to the dynamic array using `add_frame()`. It updates the signal strength indicator based on recent symbol data using `update_signal_strength()` and retrieves frames based on certain criteria using `get_frames()`. The function also handles errors and exceptions by calling `error_handler()`.

This implementation uses a mutex to lock the access to the dynamic array of frames to ensure thread-safety. It also includes error handling and exception handling mechanisms to gracefully handle errors and exceptions that may occur during the execution of the program.']

