To manage dynamic memory allocation for an array of `NFAPI_LOG_PDU_T` structures in a high-throughput logging system, you can use a thread-safe and concurrent-access-friendly data structure such as a lock-free linked list or a bounded buffer. This will allow multiple threads to access the logging framework without causing race conditions or other synchronization issues.

Here is an example of how this could be implemented:
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Define a lock-free linked list data structure for storing log messages
typedef struct log_message {
    int log_type;
    int ue_id;
    char* log_content;
    long timestamp;
    int severity;
} log_message_t;

// Define a thread-safe logging function that takes a log message and adds it to the linked list
void log(log_message_t* msg) {
    // Acquire lock on the linked list
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list_lock);

    // Add log message to the end of the linked list
    log_message_t* new_msg = malloc(sizeof(log_message_t));
    memcpy(new_msg, msg, sizeof(log_message_t));
    new_msg->next = NULL;
    if (list_head == NULL) {
        list_head = new_msg;
    } else {
        log_message_t* curr = list_head;
        while (curr->next != NULL) {
            curr = curr->next;
        }
        curr->next = new_msg;
    }

    // Release lock on the linked list
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list_lock);
}

// Define a thread-safe function for flushing the log messages to disk
void flush_logs() {
    // Acquire lock on the linked list
    pthread_mutex_lock(&list_lock);

    // Iterate over the linked list and write each message to disk
    log_message_t* curr = list_head;
    while (curr != NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "%d %d %s\n", curr->log_type, curr->ue_id, curr->log_content);
        curr = curr->next;
    }

    // Release lock on the linked list
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&list_lock);
}
```
In this example, the `log()` function takes a log message and adds it to the end of a lock-free linked list. The `flush_logs()` function iterates over the linked list and writes each log message to disk. The linked list is protected by a mutex to ensure thread safety.

To support customizable logging levels and formats, you can define separate functions for each logging level (e.g., `log_debug()`, `log_info()`, etc.) that take the appropriate log message structure as an argument. These functions can then be used to add log messages to the linked list in a way that is optimized for performance based on the current logging level and format.

To balance logging granularity with performance, you can use a hierarchical logging system where more detailed logs are only generated at certain levels of verbosity (e.g., `log_debug()` may only be called when the logging level is set to debug). This will reduce the amount of log data that needs to be processed and written to disk during peak logging periods, while still allowing for detailed logging in cases where it is necessary.

To minimize memory fragmentation and overhead, you can use a bounded buffer data structure (e.g., a circular buffer) to store log messages instead of an unbounded linked list. This will prevent the linked list from growing uncontrollably during peak logging periods, while still allowing for efficient write operations.

Overall, this implementation should be scalable to handle increased logging demands without degrading application responsiveness, while also ensuring thread safety and preventing race conditions.']

