### 2.3.4 Example: Hash Map with Custom Types

In this example, we'll create a hash map that uses custom types as the keys and values. We'll use a simple `struct` to represent the key and value, and a function to compare the keys for equality.

Here is an example of how you might define a hash map with custom types:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include "hashmap.h"

// Define a struct to represent a key and value in the hash map
struct my_key_value {
    char* key;
    int value;
};

// Define a function to compare two keys for equality
bool compare_keys(const void* key1, const void* key2) {
    return strcmp(((struct my_key_value*)key1)->key, ((struct my_key_value*)key2)->key) == 0;
}

int main() {
    // Create a new hash map with the custom types
    struct hashmap* my_hash = hashmap_create(compare_keys);

    // Add some keys and values to the hash map
    struct my_key_value key1 = {"hello", 1};
    hashmap_add(my_hash, &key1, &key1.value);

    struct my_key_value key2 = {"world", 2};
    hashmap_add(my_hash, &key2, &key2.value);

    // Look up a value by key
    struct my_key_value lookup_key = {"hello", 0};
    int* value = hashmap_get(my_hash, &lookup_key);
    printf("Value for hello: %d\n", *value);

    // Remove a key from the hash map
    hashmap_remove(my_hash, &key1);

    // Clean up memory used by the hash map
    hashmap_destroy(my_hash);

    return 0;
}
```
