    pthread_mutex_unlock(&crc_list_lock);
}

// Deserialize a list of NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structures from a file and add them to the current list
void deserialize_crcs(const char* filename) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&crc_list_lock);
    FILE* f = fopen(filename, "rb");
    if (f == NULL) {
        printf("Error: unable to open file for reading\n");
        exit(-1);
    }
    int num_crcs; // the number of elements in the list to deserialize
    fread(&num_crcs, sizeof(int), 1, f); // read the number of elements from the file
    for (int i = 0; i < num_crcs; i++) { // for each element in the list
        NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T crc; // a temporary structure to hold the deserialized data
        fread(&crc, sizeof(NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T), 1, f); // read the next element from the file
        add_crc(&crc); // add the deserialized structure to the current list
    }
    fclose(f);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&crc_list_lock);
}
```
This implementation uses a mutex to protect access to the list of structures from multiple threads, and provides methods for adding, removing, and accessing elements in the list. The `serialize_crcs` method writes the current state of the list to a file using `fwrite`, while the `deserialize_crcs` method reads the state from a file using `fread`.

### Thread-safe collections

Another approach is to use thread-safe collections that provide synchronized access to their elements. For example, you can use a `java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList`, which provides a snapshot of its current state at the moment in time when it is accessed by another thread. This means that any changes made to the list after the snapshot was created will not be visible to other threads until they access the list again.

Here's an example of how you could use this approach:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.CopyOnWriteArrayList;

public class ThreadSafeListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        CopyOnWriteArrayList<Integer> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();

        // add some elements to the list
        list.add(1);
        list.add(2);
        list.add(3);

        // start a separate thread that will modify the list
        Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
            // sleep for 5 seconds to allow other threads to access the list before modifying it
            try {
                Thread.sleep(5000);
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
            list.add(4);
        });
        t.start();

        // wait for the separate thread to finish modifying the list before accessing it again
        t.join();

        // print out all elements in the list, including any that were added by the other thread
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}
```
This example creates a `CopyOnWriteArrayList` and adds some initial elements to it. It then starts a separate thread that modifies the list after sleeping for 5 seconds to allow other threads to access the list before modifying it. The main thread waits for the separate thread to finish modifying the list before accessing it again, and then prints out all elements in the list, including any that were added by the other thread.

### Blocking queues

A third approach is to use a blocking queue, which allows one or more threads to put and take elements from the queue in a first-in-first-out (FIFO) order, waiting if necessary for an element to become available. This can be useful when you need to pass data between threads in a predictable way.

Here's an example of how you could use this approach:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class BlockingQueueExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BlockingQueue<Integer> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

        // add some elements to the queue
        queue.put(1);
        queue.put(2);
        queue.put(3);

        // start a separate thread that will take an element from the queue and print it
        Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println(queue.take()); // prints 1
        });
        t.start();

        // wait for the separate thread to finish taking an element from the queue before adding another one
        t.join();

        // add another element to the queue and take it from the other thread
        queue.put(4);
        System.out.println(queue.take()); // prints 4
    }
}
```
This example creates a `BlockingQueue` and adds some initial elements to it. It then starts a separate thread that takes an element from the queue using the `take()` method, which blocks until an element becomes available. The main thread waits for the separate thread to finish taking an element from the queue before adding another one, and then adds another element to the queue and takes it from the other thread.

### Synchronized collections

Finally, you can use synchronized collections that provide a lock on the collection for all access operations. This ensures that only one thread can modify the collection at any given time, which can be useful when you need to ensure consistency of the data in the collection.

Here's an example of how you could use this approach:
```java
import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.Collections;

public class SynchronizedCollectionExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Collection<Integer> collection = Collections.synchronizedCollection(new ArrayList<>());

        // add some elements to the collection
        collection.add(1);
        collection.add(2);
        collection.add(3);

        // start a separate thread that will modify the collection
        Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
            synchronized (collection) {
                collection.add(4);
            }
        });
        t.start();

        // wait for the separate thread to finish modifying the collection before accessing it again
        t.join();

        // print out all elements in the collection, including any that were added by the other thread
        System.out.println(collection);
    }
}
```
This example creates a `Collection` that is synchronized using the `synchronizedCollection()` method, which provides a lock on the collection for all access operations. The main thread adds some initial elements to the collection and then starts a separate thread that modifies the collection after sleeping for 5 seconds to allow other threads to access the collection before modifying it. The main thread waits for the separate thread to finish modifying the collection before accessing it again, and then prints out all elements in the collection, including any that were added by the other thread.

In summary, you can use one of these approaches or a combination of them to handle concurrent access to a collection in Java. The choice of approach depends on your specific requirements and the characteristics of the data being stored in the collection.']

