The `mac_rx_pool_acquire()` function acquires an element from the pool, decrementing the pool's size if necessary. It first locks the mutex to ensure thread-safe access to the pool, then checks if the pool is empty and handles the case of overflow accordingly. Finally, it waits on the semaphore until an element becomes available, and returns the acquired element.

The `mac_rx_pool_release()` function releases an element back into the pool, incrementing its size if necessary. It first unlocks the mutex to ensure thread-safe access to the pool, then increments the pool's size if necessary, and signals the semaphore to wake up any threads waiting on it.

The `mac_rx_pool_resize()` function resizes the pool to a new size specified by the `new_size` parameter. It allocates memory for the new pool using `malloc()`, copies over all the elements from the old pool, and updates the pool's size variable. If the new size is smaller than the old size, it also frees any unnecessary elements in the old pool.

