In this example, we have defined a `Server` class that contains a `connection_list` array of `Connection` objects and a `message_queue` array of integers. The `connection_list` is used to keep track of the client connections and their corresponding states, while the `message_queue` is used to store messages received from clients for later processing.

The `Server` class also contains two mutexes: one for the `connection_list` and another for the `message_queue`. This is necessary because multiple threads may access these data structures simultaneously, and we want to ensure that they are accessed in a thread-safe manner.

The `accept_connections()` function is called when the server starts up to start listening for incoming connections. It creates a new thread for each connection that is established, and passes the thread the socket descriptor of the connection as well as a pointer to the `Server` object itself. This allows the new thread to call the `process_connection()` function on the `Server` object, which will handle all of the processing for that particular connection.

The `process_connection()` function is where most of the work takes place in terms of handling client connections. It first checks if the connection is still alive by calling the `is_alive()` function and closing it if not. Then it calls the `recv_message()` function to receive a message from the client, which will block until a message is received or an error occurs. If there was an error, it logs an error message and closes the connection. Otherwise, it checks if the message is a "hello" message (i.e., the first message received from the client). If it is not, it sends an error message back to the client and disconnects. If it is, it sends a "welcome" message back to the client and starts a new thread for handling messages from that client.

The `handle_messages()` function is where the actual message processing takes place. It loops indefinitely until the connection is closed, checking if there are any messages available in the `message_queue` array. If there is, it processes them one at a time by calling the `process_message()` function and then removes them from the queue.

The `process_message()` function simply logs the message to the console. In a real-world application, this would likely involve some more complex processing or analysis of the data received from the client.

The `close_connection()` function is called when the connection is closed by either the client or the server. It removes the connection from the `connection_list` array and closes the socket descriptor.']

